WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:08.690
If I ask you, do you need to change?

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.599
And you said to me, "No", I'd be

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:11.599
worried, you know?

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:12.599
Like you think you've figured

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:13.599
everything out, like emotionally,

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:14.599
professionally, all your

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:16.119
relationships, everything's golden.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:17.450
Oh, OK.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.500
How's that working out for you, you

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.500
know?

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.620
I think the answer is always yes,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.340
which is to view ourselves or

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.710
our organizations as works in

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:25.710
progress.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.710
You know, that is an infinite

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:27.710
mindset.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:28.710
Infinite mindset is fundamentally

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.070
constant improvement.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.050
So, you know, there's big change,

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.050
there's little change, there's

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.400
tweaks, there's dramatic change.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.370
Sometimes it's reacting to changing

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.970
cultures, changes

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.670
in culture, politics, technology,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.130
you know, sometimes the change is

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.720
our own, sometimes we have to react

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.720
to it.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.720
But I think the honest

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.769
answer is yes, we have to change.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:47.769
There's always opportunity to

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:54.940
improve something.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.320
So there's this notion in businesses

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.730
that people fear change,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:00:59.860
which is just fundamentally not

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:00.860
true.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.660
People fear sudden change,

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.470
you know?

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.040
But incremental change is not

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.040
threatening.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.170
People fear change

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.040
that threatens them, right?

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.900
And very often the way we change

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.860
manage is we

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.180
do lots of PowerPoint presentations

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.180
about what's coming.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.180
"Don't worry,

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.180
and we have all these things," and

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.390
we treat it very rationally.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.390
And what we're ignoring is the

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:21.490
emotional response.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.030
Now there are some people who

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.400
love change.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.960
It's an infinite minded

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.210
thing to embrace uncertainty,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.090
to see opportunity in surprise.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.860
For some people, especially people

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.850
who have been who have gotten

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.630
good at doing something for 10

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.000
years, 20 years,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.210
that all of a sudden you say you're

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.780
going to change it, they fear.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.630
Again, there's that emotional word.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.210
They FEAR that

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.060
they won't know how to or

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.490
it will set them back.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.580
And so no number of rational

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.530
assurances will

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.530
help them get over that.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.229
At some point we have

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:02.729
to just let them go through the

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:04.659
process.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.580
The law of diffusion is a theory

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.000
that was proposed in the

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.319
50s or 60s by Emmett Rogers,

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:14.830
and basically all populations shift

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.122
across the standard deviation of the

00:02:16.122 --> 00:02:17.122
old bell curve.

00:02:17.122 --> 00:02:18.209
If you have high performers, you

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:19.409
have low performers, et cetera,

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:20.409
stuff like that.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:21.730
And with a law of diffusion tells

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.439
us, is that the first two and a half

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:24.760
percent of your population or your

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.760
innovators?

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.760
These are your big idea

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.760
people.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.760
Elon Musk, Steve Jobs,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.760
right?

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.760
Then you have 13 and a half percent

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.069
of your population that are your

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:33.069
early adopters.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.459
These are the people who are willing

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:36.379
to

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:37.870
pay a premium, suffer an

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.510
inconvenience, extend the extra

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.549
energy to be a part of something

00:02:40.549 --> 00:02:42.069
that reflects their own beliefs.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:43.400
Stand in line to see Star Wars a

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.500
week before it comes out.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.061
Even though you can just wait two or

00:02:46.061 --> 00:02:47.061
three weeks and buy a ticket and go

00:02:47.061 --> 00:02:48.061
in.

00:02:48.061 --> 00:02:49.280
For them, it's worth it.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.280
Right?

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.280
And then you have your early and

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.900
late majority who are

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.129
more cynical, more practical.

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:55.129
"What's in it for me?

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:56.129
What guarantee

00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:57.129
am I going to have?

00:02:57.129 --> 00:02:58.129
What happens if

00:02:58.129 --> 00:02:59.129
it goes wrong?

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:00.129
Will I get my money

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:01.129
back?"

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:02.129
Like that kind of stuff.

00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:03.129
You know?

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:04.129
You ever seen the YouTube video of

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:05.129
the guy dancing solo

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:06.129
and the early adopters come

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:07.129
and then the next.

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:08.129
Everyone's

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:09.129
coming, and that's a movement, right?

00:03:09.129 --> 00:03:10.129
That's a great example of how it

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:11.129
works.

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:12.129
Now, amplify that to an

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:13.129
organization.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:14.129
The innovator is the person who came

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:15.129
up with the idea, the person who

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:16.129
first started dancing and other

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:17.129
people said, "You're an idiot."

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:18.129
Right?

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:19.510
But a small group said, "I mean,

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.779
but it's funny.

00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:21.779
You know, we'll give it a try."

00:03:21.779 --> 00:03:22.779
And

00:03:22.779 --> 00:03:23.779
then before you know it, it's a

00:03:23.779 --> 00:03:24.779
perfect example of how law of

00:03:24.779 --> 00:03:25.779
diffusions work and how tipping

00:03:25.779 --> 00:03:26.989
points work.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:28.519
When we try to effect

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:29.840
change inside an organization we

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:30.900
obviously want to affect the bill

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.049
won't affect the majority of the

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:33.489
company.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.689
But that's not how change happens

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:37.709
because the majority will not

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:39.049
try something until someone has

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:40.049
tried it first.

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:41.049
They're risk

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:42.209
intolerant.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:43.209
And so what you actually want to do

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:45.430
is aim for the early adopters.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.769
You aim for the people who go right,

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:47.819
"It's not perfect.

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:49.760
I'll give it a try."

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.760
And if you

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:51.959
can get that 15

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:55.560
to 18 percent market penetration,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:56.680
it just tips.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.099
Again, because someone else has

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:00.760
tried it, it's happened.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.739
And so the way you affect change in

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:04.029
an organization is not to thrust it

00:04:04.029 --> 00:04:05.359
upon everybody because you're going

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:06.739
to get massive resistance from the

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:09.379
majority, but rather

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:10.860
identify the pockets of early

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.849
adopters, individuals and

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:14.639
teams, that are willing to try

00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:17.100
this new thing that you want to try,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.680
get the kinks out, and then before

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.739
you know it what ends up happening

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:21.280
is the majority starts getting angry

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.500
like, "Why weren't we given this?"

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.659
Well, that's called demand.

00:04:23.659 --> 00:04:24.860
We love that.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.370
So it's about building demand and

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.420
creating that tipping point.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.590
That's a more effective way to

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.689
create sticky change.

